Crane Lifting Safety, Critical-Control Verification and AI-Powered EHS Management
A crane operator is preparing to lift a heavy steel structure onto the upper level of a construction project.

The lifting plan has been approved. The operator is competent. The equipment inspection records are available. The rigging team has completed its checks.

Everything appears ready.

But what happens if the ground conditions have changed after heavy rain? What if another contractor enters the lifting zone? What if the actual lifting radius differs from the approved plan?

A single overlooked change can turn a routine lifting operation into a serious incident.

Crane lifting safety is not simply about having the right documents. It is about ensuring that critical safety controls are effective before and throughout every lift.

Across high-rise construction, infrastructure development, power plants, industrial facilities and large EPC projects, lifting operations remain among the activities requiring the most rigorous planning and supervision.

Digital EHS management systems and AI-assisted safety intelligence can help organisations strengthen these processes by connecting risk assessments, lifting permits, inspections, contractor coordination and incident learning.

However, technology must support—not replace—competent people, engineering controls and physical verification.

Why Are Crane Lifting Operations High-Risk in Construction?
Crane lifting operations combine heavy suspended loads, complex equipment, changing site conditions and multiple work teams.

An incident may result from one major failure or several smaller control failures occurring together.

Common crane lifting hazards include:

Crane Overturning and Structural Failure
Inadequate ground bearing capacity, incorrect outrigger arrangements, unsuitable crane configuration or exceeding rated capacity can contribute to crane instability or failure.

Ground conditions must be assessed for the actual loads imposed by the crane. Particular attention is required near excavations, underground services, backfilled ground and temporary structures.

Falling or Uncontrolled Loads
Incorrect rigging, unsuitable lifting accessories, poorly assessed centres of gravity and uncontrolled load movement can cause dropped loads or serious struck-by incidents.

Rigging arrangements must be suitable for the load geometry, attachment points and planned lifting method.

Workers Entering Lifting Zones
Workers, visitors or other contractors may enter hazardous areas during lifting operations.

Effective exclusion zones require physical segregation, clear communication and active supervision. Warning signs alone may be insufficient.

Contact with Overhead Power Lines
Crane booms, suspended loads and lifting accessories can create serious electrical hazards when operating near energised overhead lines.

Applicable legal clearance requirements, isolation arrangements and other necessary protective measures must be established before work begins.

Simultaneous Operations and Contractor Conflicts
Multiple contractors may perform lifting, scaffolding, excavation, vehicle movement and structural installation activities in the same work area.

Without effective coordination, one activity can introduce hazards into another.

Weather and Environmental Conditions
Wind, lightning, rain and reduced visibility can affect lifting safety.

Wind limits must reflect the equipment manufacturer's instructions, the lifting plan and the characteristics of the load. Large surface-area loads can be particularly sensitive to wind.

What Are the Critical Safety Controls for Crane Lifting?
Critical controls are measures that prevent a serious incident or reduce its consequences.

For lifting operations, these controls must be selected through an appropriate risk assessment and verified at the worksite.

Seven essential control areas deserve particular attention.

1. Lifting Planning and Engineering Assessment
Every lifting operation should be planned by competent personnel, with a level of detail appropriate to its complexity and risk.

The lifting plan should address the load weight, centre of gravity, lifting radius, crane configuration, rigging arrangement, load path, landing location and foreseeable hazards.

Complex or unusual lifts may require additional engineering assessment.

2. Crane Suitability and Ground Stability
The selected crane must be suitable for the intended lifting operation.

Teams must verify rated capacity for the planned configuration, ground support arrangements, outriggers, mats and proximity to excavations or underground structures.

3. Lifting Equipment and Rigging Integrity
Slings, shackles, hooks, lifting beams and attachment points must be suitable and inspected as required.

The rigging arrangement should account for sling angles, load distribution, sharp edges and potential movement.

4. Competency and Supervision
Crane operators, riggers, signal persons and lifting supervisors must have the competencies appropriate to their duties.

Responsibilities, communication protocols and stop-work authority should be understood before the lift begins.

5. Exclusion Zones and Load-Path Management
The lifting zone must be controlled to prevent unauthorised exposure to suspended loads and moving equipment.

The load path should minimise risks to workers and surrounding operations.

6. Permit-to-Work and Risk Assessment
Where a lifting permit is required, it should connect the approved lifting plan with relevant risk assessments, supporting documents, worksite conditions and authorisation responsibilities.

Permit approval is not a substitute for verifying actual conditions.

7. Emergency Preparedness and Stop-Work Authority
Emergency arrangements should reflect the risks of the operation.

Personnel must be empowered to stop work when unsafe conditions arise or when the operation deviates materially from the approved plan.

Why Can a Completed Lifting Checklist Still Leave Workers Exposed?
A completed checklist confirms that information has been recorded. It does not automatically prove that every safety control remains effective.

Consider a lifting operation where the exclusion zone was verified at 8:00 AM.

At 10:30 AM, another contractor temporarily removes part of the barrier to move materials.

The original inspection record may still show the exclusion zone as satisfactory.

The actual condition has changed.

This is why effective lifting safety requires ongoing verification, communication and reassessment when circumstances change.

Digital EHS workflows can support this process by recording observations, assigning responsibilities, escalating corrective actions and maintaining traceability.

How Can AI Improve Crane Lifting Safety?
AI-powered EHS technologies can support crane lifting safety through better information analysis, faster identification of recurring issues and improved management visibility.

Their usefulness depends on appropriate configuration, reliable data and human oversight.

AI-Assisted Hazard Identification
AI-assisted analysis can help identify recurring hazards across inspection reports, near misses and risk assessments.

For example, repeated observations involving rigging defects, exclusion-zone breaches or inadequate lifting-area segregation may indicate systemic weaknesses requiring management attention.

Predictive Safety Analytics
Predictive analytics can examine historical patterns involving inspection deficiencies, contractor performance, permit deviations and incident trends.

These patterns may help safety teams prioritise reviews and preventive interventions.

Predictive analytics cannot guarantee that a crane incident will occur or that an individual lift is safe.

Computer Vision for Construction Safety
Where appropriately deployed and validated, computer vision may assist with detecting certain visible conditions, such as people entering restricted lifting areas.

However, computer vision cannot independently verify load calculations, ground bearing capacity or rigging suitability.

Detection performance can also be affected by camera positioning, visibility, lighting and obstruction.

Connected EHS Data and Management Dashboards
Connecting permits, risk assessments, inspections, incidents and corrective actions can help organisations identify overdue controls and recurring deficiencies across multiple projects.

The value comes from making safety information accessible to the right people at the right time.

How Does Digital Permit-to-Work Software Support Lifting Operations?
Digital Permit-to-Work software can support lifting authorisation through structured workflows and traceable approvals.

A lifting-permit process may include:

Submission of the lifting plan and task risk assessment.

Verification of required supporting equipment and competency records.

Review of site conditions and simultaneous operations.

Approval by authorised personnel.

Confirmation of field-level safety prerequisites.

Recording of changes, suspension, reassessment and closure.

Digital systems can reduce fragmented records and make approval responsibilities more visible.

However, a digitally approved permit does not replace a competent lifting supervisor or physical site verification.

